      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 91 and 60. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers together. I remember that when multiplying, you can break down one of the numbers into smaller parts to make the multiplication easier. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. Let me try that.\n\nFirst, 91 can be broken down into 90 + 1. So, if I multiply 90 by 60 and then add 1 times 60, that should give me the same result. Let me check that. 90 times 60 is 5400, and 1 times 60 is 60. Adding those together, 5400 + 60 equals 5460. Wait, is that right? Let me verify.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do the multiplication directly. 91 times 60. Let's see, 90 times 60 is 5400, and 1 times 60 is 60. So adding those gives 5400 + 60 = 5460. That seems correct. But let me make sure I didn't make a mistake in the order of operations. Multiplication is commutative, so 91 times 60 is the same as 60 times 91. Either way, the result should be the same. So 5460 is the answer.\n\nWait, but let me think again. Maybe there's another way to approach this. Let's see. 91 is 90 + 1, so 91*60 = (90 + 1)*60. Using the distributive property, that's 90*60 + 1*60. 90*60 is 5400, and 1*60 is 60. So again, 5400 + 60 = 5460. Yep, same result. So that's another way to confirm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the standard multiplication method. Let's write 91 and 60 vertically. 60 is above 91. Multiply 60 by 90 first. 60*90 is 5400. Then multiply 60 by 1, which is 60. Then add those two results together: 5400 + 60 = 5460. Same answer again. Okay, so all methods lead to 5460. I think that's solid.\n\nWait, just to make sure there's no trick here. The question is asking for the product of 91 and 60. Since both numbers are positive, their product should be positive. 91 is 91, 60 is 60, so multiplying them gives 5460. That seems straightforward. No negatives or anything like that. So I don't see any issues here. I think that's the correct answer.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nThe product of \\(91\\) and \\(60\\) is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n91 \\times 60 = (90 + 1) \\times 60 = 90 \\times 60 + 1 \\times 60 = 5400 + 60 = 5460\n\\]\n\nAlternatively, using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n91 \\times 60 = 90 \\times 60 + 1 \\times 60 = 5400 + 60 = 5460\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the result. The final answer is \\(\\boxed{5460}\\).\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
