      "index": 0,
      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 26 and 11. Hmm, let me think. Multiplication is just adding a number to itself a certain number of times. But maybe there's a smarter way to do this without having to do all the addition. Let me recall some multiplication tricks.\n\nFirst, I remember that multiplying by 10 is easy because you just add a zero. So, 26 times 10 is 260. Then, since 11 is 10 plus 1, maybe I can break down the multiplication into two parts. Let me try that.\n\n26 times 11. Let's write that as 26 times (10 + 1). Using the distributive property, that would be (26 times 10) + (26 times 1). \n\nCalculating each part separately: 26 times 10 is 260, and 26 times 1 is 26. Then, adding those two results together: 260 + 26. Hmm, 260 plus 26. Let me add them step by step. 260 + 20 is 280, and then 26 more is 306. So, 260 + 26 is 306. \n\nWait, that seems straightforward. Let me check if there's another way to do this. Maybe using the standard multiplication method. Let's see:\n\n26\n\u00d7 11\n------\n\nMultiply 26 by 1 (the ones place of 11). That's 26. Then, multiply 26 by 10 (the tens place of 11). That's 260. Then, add those two products together: 26 + 260. \n\n26 + 260. Let's add the tens and ones separately. 260 + 26. 260 + 20 is 280, and 26 + 20 is 46. So, 280 + 46. Let's add those: 280 + 40 is 320, and 320 + 6 is 326. Yep, same result. \n\nSo, both methods give me 306. That seems consistent. Let me just verify once more. Maybe using another approach. Let's think of 26 as 20 + 6. Then, 20 times 11 is 220, and 6 times 11 is 66. Adding those together: 220 + 66. Let's compute that. 220 + 60 is 280, and 280 + 6 is 286. So, 220 + 66 is 286. Then, 286 + 20 is 306. Same answer again. \n\nOkay, so all three methods\u2014distributive property, standard multiplication, and breaking down the number\u2014give me 306. I think that's solid. Let me just check if there's any chance I made a mistake in any of these steps. \n\nFirst method: 26 times 10 is 260, 26 times 1 is 26. 260 + 26. 260 + 20 is 280, 26 + 20 is 46. 280 + 46. 280 + 40 is 320, 320 + 6 is 326. Correct. \n\nSecond method: 26 times 11. 26 times 10 is 260, 26 times 1 is 26. 260 + 26. 260 + 20 is 280, 26 + 20 is 46. 280 + 46. 280 + 40 is 320, 320 + 6 is 326. Correct. \n\nThird method: 26 as 20 + 6. 20 times 11 is 220, 6 times 11 is 66. 220 + 66. 220 + 60 is 280, 280 + 6 is 286. 286 + 20 is 306. Correct. \n\nAll three methods confirm that the product is 306. I don't see any errors in the calculations. So, I'm confident that the answer is 306.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nTo find the product of \\(26 \\times 11\\), we can use the distributive property or break down the numbers:\n\n1. **Distributive Property**:  \n   \\(26 \\times 11 = 26 \\times (10 + 1) = 26 \\times 10 + 26 \\times 1 = 260 + 26 = 306\\).\n\n2. **Standard Multiplication**:  \n   \\(26 \\times 11\\):  \n   - \\(26 \\times 10 = 260\\),  \n   - \\(26 \\times 1 = 26\\),  \n   - Adding these results: \\(260 + 26 = 306\\).\n\n3. **Alternative Breakdown**:  \n   \\(26 = 20 + 6\\), so:  \n   \\(20 \\times 11 = 220\\),  \n   \\(6 \\times 11 = 66\\),  \n   \\(220 + 66 = 306\\).\n\nAll methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{306}\\).\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
