      "index": 0,
      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 30 and 63. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers together. Let me recall how to do this. \n\nFirst, I remember that when multiplying, you can break down one of the numbers into smaller parts to make the multiplication easier. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. Let's see, 30 times 63. If I split 30 into 30 and 10, that might help. So, 30*63 = (30*10)*6. \n\nCalculating that, 30*10 is 300, and 30*6 is 180. Then, multiplying those two results together: 300*6. Let me do that. 300*6 is 1800. So, 30*63 is 1800. \n\nWait, let me check that again. 30*60 is 1800, and 30*3 is 90. Adding those together: 1800 + 90 = 1890. But 30*63 is 1890. Hmm, that's the same as before. So, 30*63 is indeed 1890. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's see, 63 is 60 + 3. So, 30*60 is 1800, and 30*3 is 90. Adding those gives 1890. Yep, same answer. \n\nIs there another way to approach this? Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's try that. \n\n30  \n\u00d7 63  \n------  \nFirst, multiply 30 by 3: 30*3 = 90.  \nThen, multiply 30 by 60: 30*60 = 1800.  \nNow, add those two results: 90 + 1800 = 1890. Same answer. \n\nWait, so all methods lead to the same result. That gives me confidence that 30*63 is indeed 1890. \n\nLet me just verify once more. 30 times 60 is 1800, and 30 times 3 is 90. Adding them together: 1800 + 90 = 1890. Yep, that's correct. \n\nI don't think I made any mistakes here. The key was breaking down 63 into 60 + 3 to simplify the multiplication. That's a good strategy when dealing with larger numbers. \n\nSo, the final answer should be 1890. I can't see any errors in my calculations. All methods converge to the same result. Therefore, I'm confident that 30 multiplied by 63 is 1890.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nTo find the product of \\(30\\) and \\(63\\), we can use the distributive property by breaking down \\(30\\) into \\(30 + 10\\):\n\n\\[\n30 \\times 63 = (30 + 10) \\times 63\n\\]\n\nApplying the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n30 \\times 63 = 30 \\times 60 + 30 \\times 3\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n\\[\n30 \\times 60 = 1800 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 30 \\times 3 = 90\n\\]\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n1800 + 90 = 1890\n\\]\n\nAlternatively, using another method:\n\n\\[\n63 = 60 + 3 \\implies 30 \\times 63 = 30 \\times (60 + 3) = 30 \\times 60 + 30 \\times 3 = 1800 + 90 = 1890\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{1890}\\).\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
