      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 16 and 31. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers together. Let me recall how to do this. \n\nFirst, I remember that when multiplying two numbers, you can break them down into smaller parts. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. Let's see, 16 times 31. If I can express 31 as 30 + 1, then maybe I can multiply 16 by 30 and 16 by 1 separately, and then add those two results together. That should make the calculation easier.\n\nLet me try that. 16 times 30 is straightforward. 16 times 30 is 480. Then, 16 times 1 is 16. So adding those two together, 480 + 16. Let me compute that. 480 plus 16. Well, 480 plus 10 is 490, and then plus 6 more is 496. So that would be 496. \n\nWait, let me check if there's another way to do this. Maybe using another method. Let's see. 16 times 31. Let's think of 31 as 30 + 1. So 16 times (30 + 1) is 16*30 + 16*1. Which is 480 + 16. Again, same result. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's write 16 and 31 vertically. \n\n16\n\u00d7 31\n------\nMultiply 16 by 1 (the ones place of 31). That's 16*1=16. Then, multiply 16 by 30 (the tens place of 31). That's 16*30=480. Now, add those two results together: 16 + 480. Let's add them. 16 + 480. 480 + 16. 480 + 10 is 490, and then +6 is 496. Yep, same answer. \n\nHmm, seems like all methods lead to the same result. So I can be confident that the product of 16 and 31 is 496. \n\nWait, just to make sure I didn't make any mistakes in the steps. Let me verify each part again. \n\nFirst, 16 times 30. 16*30. 16*3 is 48, and 16*10 is 160. Adding them gives 480. Correct. Then 16 times 1. 16*1 is 16. Correct. Adding 480 and 16 gives 496. Correct. \n\nAlternatively, 16*31. 16*30 is 480, 16*1 is 16. 480+16=496. Yep. \n\nAnother way: 16*30=480, 16*1=16. 480+16=496. \n\nAlternatively, 16*30=480, 16*1=16. 480+16=496. \n\nSo all methods confirm that the product is 496. \n\nI think that's solid. I don't see any errors in the reasoning. So the answer should be 496.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nTo find the product of \\(16\\) and \\(31\\), we can use the distributive property by breaking down \\(31\\) into \\(30 + 1\\):\n\n\\[\n16 \\times 31 = 16 \\times (30 + 1) = 16 \\times 30 + 16 \\times 1\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\n\\[\n16 \\times 30 = 480 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 16 \\times 1 = 16\n\\]\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n480 + 16 = 496\n\\]\n\nVerification using another method (standard multiplication):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c@{}c@{}c}\n & 1 & 6 \\\\\n\\times & 3 & 1 \\\\\n\\hline\n & 1 & 6 \\\\\n+ & 3 & 8 \\\\\n\\hline\n & 4 & 9 & 6 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the product is \\(496\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{496}\n\\]\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
