      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 100 and 75. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers together. I remember that when you multiply a number by 100, you just add two zeros to the end. But wait, 75 isn't a multiple of 100, so maybe I need to break it down. Let me try that.\n\nFirst, 100 times 75. Let's see, 100 times 70 is 7,000, and 100 times 5 is 500. Then, adding those together: 7,000 + 500. That's 7,500. So, 100 times 75 is 7,500. Let me check that again. 100 times 70 is 7,000, and 100 times 5 is 500. 7,000 + 500 is indeed 7,500. Yeah, that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's see, 75 times 100. Well, 75 times 100 is 7500. That's straightforward. So, both methods give me the same result. So, the product of 100 and 75 is 7,500.\n\nWait, just to make sure I didn't make a mistake in the first method. Let me verify again. 100 times 70 is 7,000. 100 times 5 is 500. Adding them together: 7,000 + 500. 7,000 + 500 is 7,500. Yep, that's correct. So, 100 times 75 is 7,500.\n\nAlternatively, if I use the distributive property, 100 times 75 can be written as 100*(70 + 5). Then, 100*70 is 7,000 and 100*5 is 500. Adding them gives 7,500. That's another way to see it. So, all methods lead to the same answer.\n\nI think that's solid. So, the product of 100 and 75 is 7,500.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nThe product of 100 and 75 is calculated by multiplying the two numbers together. Breaking down 75 into 70 + 5, we get:\n\n\\[\n100 \\times 75 = 100 \\times (70 + 5) = 100 \\times 70 + 100 \\times 5 = 7000 + 500 = 7500\n\\]\n\nAlternatively, using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n100 \\times 75 = 100 \\times (70 + 5) = 100 \\times 70 + 100 \\times 5 = 7000 + 500 = 7500\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the result. The final answer is \\boxed{7500}.\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
